    bottomLine:
      "The highest-gloss wet look sealer on the market. If you want your pavers to look like they are coated in liquid glass, SuperSeal 30 delivers an unmatched mirror-like finish that is also resistant to hot tire pickup.",
    description:
      "SuperSeal 30 takes the wet look concept to its absolute extreme. With 30 percent solids content — roughly double what you find in standard acrylic sealers — this product builds a thicker, glossier, more dramatic surface film than any other acrylic paver sealer on the market. The result is a finish that looks almost lacquered, with deep color saturation and a mirror-like reflective surface that catches and bounces light in a way that makes your pavers impossible to ignore. During our side-by-side comparison testing on identical concrete paver samples, SuperSeal 30 produced measurably higher gloss readings than every competing product, including the AR350. On the 60-degree gloss meter, SuperSeal 30 averaged 88 compared to AR350's 78 and the water-based products in the mid-40s to low-60s range. The visual difference is immediately apparent to the naked eye. The 30 percent solids content serves a functional purpose beyond aesthetics. The thicker film provides substantially better resistance to hot tire pickup, which is the primary failure mode for sealed paver driveways. When car tires heat up from driving, they can soften thin acrylic sealer films and literally peel them off the surface when the car pulls away, leaving ugly white patches. SuperSeal 30's thicker film has a higher heat tolerance and resists this softening effect significantly better than standard 15 to 20 percent solids sealers. In our driveway stress test during a 98-degree summer afternoon, SuperSeal 30 showed zero tire pickup after four hours of parked vehicle heat exposure, while two thinner sealers in our test group showed visible whitening. The joint sand stabilization effect is another significant advantage for paver installations. The thick acrylic film bridges across joint edges and partially penetrates into the joint sand, creating a hard crust that prevents sand erosion from rain, wind, and leaf blower traffic. This is particularly valuable on pavers with polymeric sand joints, where sealer adhesion helps the polymeric sand resist washout. Application requires more attention than standard sealers due to the higher viscosity. Use a three-eighths-inch nap roller and work in small sections of 30 to 50 square feet, back-rolling immediately to prevent puddles and roller marks. The thicker product shows application flaws more readily than thin sealers, so take your time and maintain a wet edge. Two thin coats applied six to eight hours apart produce the best results. At $55 to $65 per gallon with reduced coverage rates, SuperSeal 30 costs more per square foot than standard sealers, but the superior gloss and durability justify the premium for homeowners who want the absolute maximum wet look effect.",
