      "Safe for use around landscaping, pools, and planted areas with no chemical runoff concerns",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Wet look effect fades faster than premium sealers — expect 8-12 months on patios",
      "Color enhancement depth is noticeably less than solvent-based competitors",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best wet look sealer for budget-conscious homeowners who want noticeable color enhancement without the premium price tag. Easy to apply and easy to recoat when the effect fades.",
    description:
      "Black Diamond Stoneworks Wet Look Sealer consistently ranks among the top-selling paver sealers online, and our testing reveals a product that delivers solid performance at a very competitive price point. This water-based acrylic sealer produces a noticeable wet look enhancement on concrete pavers, brick pavers, and natural stone — colors darken by roughly 15 to 20 percent and gain a subtle satin sheen that makes the surface look freshly rained on. It is not the deepest or most dramatic wet look on the market, but for a product that costs $30 to $40 per gallon, the results are genuinely impressive. The application experience is about as simple as paver sealing gets. The product is ready to use straight from the container with no mixing, dilution, or special preparation required. Roll it on with a standard three-eighths-inch nap roller or apply with a pump sprayer, allow two to three hours between coats, and you are done. The water-based formula has virtually no odor, cleans up with soap and water, and is safe to use around pools, landscaping, and occupied properties. A first-time DIY homeowner can seal a 400-square-foot paver patio in about three hours including cleanup. The trade-off for the lower price is shorter service life. In our outdoor exposure testing, the Black Diamond sealer maintained acceptable appearance for about eight to twelve months on a patio surface before the wet look began to fade noticeably. On a driveway section with vehicle traffic, visible wear appeared within six months. This is meaningfully shorter than the two to three year lifespan of premium products like AR350 and SuperSeal 30. However, the easy recoating process — just clean and roll on a fresh coat, no stripping required — means the maintenance burden is not as heavy as the shorter lifespan might suggest. At a cost of roughly $0.10 to $0.13 per square foot per coat, annual recoating is still cheaper than a single application of a premium sealer every three years. For homeowners who do not mind a quick annual recoat, Black Diamond provides continuous wet look protection at the lowest total cost of ownership in our test group. The product also performs well as a natural stone sealer for applications like flagstone, bluestone, and travertine pavers where enhanced color and subtle sheen are desirable without the extreme high gloss that solvent-based products produce.",
  },
