        <h2 id="common-wet-look-sealer-problems" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Common Wet Look Sealer Problems and How to Prevent Them
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">White Discoloration (Blushing)</h3>
        <p>
          White clouding or blushing is the most common wet look sealer failure on pavers. It occurs when moisture becomes trapped beneath the transparent sealer film, creating a milky appearance that completely ruins the wet look effect. The moisture can come from applying sealer to pavers that have not fully dried after rain or pressure washing, from rain falling on freshly sealed pavers before the sealer cures, or from ground moisture wicking upward through the paver base and bedding sand. Prevention is straightforward: ensure pavers are completely dry before sealing, check the weather forecast for at least 24 hours of dry conditions after application, and avoid sealing during periods of high humidity. If whitening occurs, applying a thin coat of xylene to acrylic sealers can re-dissolve the film and allow trapped moisture to escape during re-curing. Severe whitening requires full stripping and reapplication.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Peeling and Delamination</h3>
        <p>
          Peeling sealer on pavers is caused by poor adhesion, which typically results from one of three conditions: applying sealer over a dirty surface with dust, pollen, or organic residue that prevents bonding; applying new sealer over existing failed sealer that has already lost adhesion; or applying excessive thickness in a single coat, which causes the sealer film to crack and delaminate as it cures. The prevention for all three is the same discipline: clean the surface thoroughly, strip any existing failed sealer, and apply thin coats. Once peeling has begun, the only remedy is full stripping and reapplication — you cannot patch a peeling sealer.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Yellowing Over Time</h3>
        <p>
          Yellowing is caused by UV degradation of the acrylic resin in the sealer film. Budget sealers that use cheaper resin blends are particularly susceptible, with noticeable yellowing appearing within 12 to 18 months of sun exposure. On light-colored pavers, yellowing transforms the clean wet look into an ugly amber tint that makes the entire paver field look dirty. The prevention is simple: use a sealer formulated with UV-stabilized, non-yellowing acrylic resins. All seven products on our recommended list use UV-stable formulations. If yellowing has already occurred, the discolored sealer must be stripped and replaced with a UV-stable product.
        </p>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best wet look sealer for pavers?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Foundation Armor AR350 is the best overall wet look paver sealer for most homeowners. It produces a deep, rich gloss that dramatically enhances paver colors while providing two to three years of UV-stable protection. For maximum gloss intensity, SuperSeal 30 delivers an even more dramatic mirror-like wet look with its 30 percent solids formulation. For an easy DIY application without chemical solvent hassles, Eagle Sealer EU1 is the best water-based wet look option.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does wet look paver sealer last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Solvent-based wet look sealers typically last two to three years on patios with moderate foot traffic and one to two years on driveways with vehicle traffic. Water-based wet look sealers last one to two years on patios and eight to twelve months on driveways. Higher-solids formulas like SuperSeal 30 can last three to four years on low-traffic surfaces. The water droplet test is the simplest way to check: sprinkle water on the surface. If it beads up, the sealer is still active. If it soaks in, it is time to recoat.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is solvent-based or water-based better for a wet look?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Solvent-based sealers produce a deeper, more dramatic wet look with 40 to 60 percent color intensification versus 15 to 25 percent from water-based products. However, water-based sealers are easier to apply, have lower odor, clean up with water, and are VOC-compliant in all states. If maximum wet look intensity is your priority and you are comfortable working with chemical solvents, solvent-based is the better choice. If ease of application and regulatory compliance matter more, water-based delivers a good wet look with significantly fewer hassles.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will wet look sealer make my pavers slippery?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Yes, glossy wet look sealers create a smooth surface film that can become dangerously slippery when wet. This is a genuine safety concern around pool decks, on sloped walkways, and in climates with frequent rain. The solution is adding a non-slip additive like polymer beads or aluminum oxide grit to the sealer before application. These additives embed in the film and create microscopic surface texture that provides traction underfoot even when wet, with only minimal reduction in gloss level.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply wet look sealer over existing sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              You can apply new wet look sealer over existing acrylic sealer that is still in good condition — meaning it is not peeling, whitening, or flaking. Clean the surface thoroughly, allow it to dry, and apply one thin coat. If the existing sealer is failing in any way, it must be stripped completely before resealing. Applying new sealer over failed sealer always results in the new product failing as well, wasting both time and money.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I prevent wet look sealer from turning white?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              White discoloration is caused by moisture trapped beneath the sealer film. Prevent it by ensuring pavers are completely dry before sealing — wait at least 48 hours after rain or pressure washing. Check the weather forecast for at least 24 hours of dry conditions after application. Apply thin coats rather than one thick coat. Avoid sealing during conditions of high humidity above 85 percent relative humidity. If some whitening does occur, a thin application of xylene over the affected area can often re-dissolve the film and allow the moisture to escape.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal pavers before or after applying polymeric sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Always install and activate polymeric sand first, then seal after the sand has fully cured. Polymeric sand requires direct water exposure to activate its binding polymers, and a sealer film would prevent this activation from occurring properly. Wait at least 24 to 48 hours after polymeric sand installation and water activation before applying your wet look sealer. This sequence ensures both the polymeric sand and the sealer perform at their best.
            </p>
          </div>
